\mt1 Psalms

\c 3

\d A psalm of David, when he fled from Absalom his son.\f + \fr 3:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq when he fled from Absalom his son \ft This is the first psalm in the entire Psalter to carry a narrative superscription. Modern scholarship is unanimous that the heading is interpretive, not biographical: it is what the NICOT commentary (deClaissé-Walford, Jacobson, Tanner, 2014) calls "the first exegetical activity" on the psalm — an ancient editor noticing verbal overlaps with the Absalom revolt narrative (2 Samuel 15–18) and connecting the prayer to that moment in David's life. Goldingay (2006) and Mays (1994) reach the same conclusion. The connection is imaginative and pastoral, not a claim about who wrote the psalm. — The Absalom story itself begins with a wound the heading does not name. Two chapters earlier (2 Samuel 13), David's son Amnon raped his half-sister Tamar; David did not punish Amnon; Tamar's full brother Absalom eventually killed Amnon and went into exile, then returned and raised the revolt the superscription invokes. Feminist exegesis, in its most mature form in Phyllis Trible and J. Cheryl Exum, documents how this superscription has functioned to flatten an arc of male violence and political crisis while erasing the woman whose trauma set the chain in motion. To historicize Psalm 3 honestly is to remember that the prayer for deliverance is being prayed inside a world where one woman's rape was the uncorrected original wound.\f*\f + \fr 3:0 \fk TEXT \fq A psalm of David \ft Modern readers reasonably ask whether the David attribution is historical. The honest scholarly answer has two layers. First, the literary form: the Hebrew superscription *lə-Dāwid* can mean "by David," "for David," "in the Davidic style," or "from the Davidic collection" — Hebraicists have defended each reading, and the preposition's ambiguity is not a flaw in the manuscript tradition but a feature of biblical Hebrew. Second, the textual transmission: the consonantal text of Psalm 3 is unusually stable across two and a half millennia. The Septuagint translation (3rd–2nd c. BCE) preserves both the psalm and its superscription faithfully; the Targums and Peshitta agree. Although Psalm 3 is not directly preserved among the Dead Sea Scrolls fragments that survive, Peter Flint's comprehensive study (*The Dead Sea Psalms Scrolls and the Book of Psalms*, Brill 1997) documents that variants in Book I (Pss 1–41) — where Psalm 3 sits — are minor compared with the late-canon fluidity of Books IV–V. Whatever questions remain about original authorship, the text in our hands is not a late forgery; it has been transmitted with consistency that is the rule rather than the exception for Book I. — A second apologetic point: when modern scholarship treats the Absalom superscription as ancient editorial commentary rather than biographical claim, this is not a discovery that undermines the psalm. It is what makes the psalm available to anyone in any crisis, not just to a Bronze Age king. The honesty about how the heading came to be increases, not decreases, the prayer's pastoral reach.\f*

\s1 Surrounded

\b
\q1
\v 1 LORD, how many\add  — how many\add* are against me.\f + \fr 3:1 \fk TEXT \fq how many \ft The Hebrew word *rabbîm* ("many") appears three times across vv.1–2 — once as a verb (*mah-rabbū*, "how they are many") and twice as a participle ("many rising," "many saying"). Adele Berlin notes that the threefold repetition functions as both grammatical and semantic parallelism: a flood of "many" closing in. Most English translations break this anaphora — KJV reads "Many there be... many are they... many there be" with three different syntactic frames. Aperto preserves the three "many"s in three consecutive lines because the structural device is doing the emotional work. The repetition IS the threat. — The Hebrew root *rabb-* will return in v.6 as *ribbəbôt* ("ten thousand") — the same root expanded. The threat grows from "many" to "myriads," but the psalmist's fear has dissolved. Same root, opposite emotional effect.\f*
\q1 Many rise up against me.\f + \fr 3:1 \fk KULTUR \fq rise up \ft *Qāmîm ʿālay* — "rising against me" — uses the Hebrew root *qûm* ("to rise, to stand"), which will return at v.7 in the imperative addressed to YHWH: "Arise!" The same verb the enemies use in their political uprising is the verb the psalmist asks God to use in response. Hossfeld and Zenger call this lexical chiasm the elegant structural device of the psalm. The prayer mirrors the threat: you rose against me — now God rise against you. NICOT identifies this as one of the most carefully constructed lexical reversals in the Psalter. The English "rise up" / "Arise" preserves the chiasm where most older translations lose it.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 2 Many are saying of me:\f + \fr 3:2 \fk KULTUR \fq there is no rescue for him in God \ft James Mays (*Psalms*, Interpretation 1994) calls this verdict "a mortally dangerous weapon against the soul. It has an ally in every crevice of doubt, anxiety, and guilt in the heart." The enemies are not denying that God exists — they are denying that God will help *this person*. In an honor-shame world, that pronouncement kills socially before any sword is drawn. To be publicly declared abandoned by your god was, in the ancient Near East, to be erased from the community: no one could safely associate with you, no one would lend, no one would aid. The psalm's entire counter-argument is built into one Hebrew word — *yəšûʿāh* ("rescue") — which the enemies deny here in v.2 and the psalmist will declare in v.8. Same word, reversed ownership.\f*
\q1 "There is no rescue\f + \fr 3:2 \fk TEXT \fq rescue \ft The Hebrew word is *yəšûʿāh*, which the King James Version translates "salvation" and most subsequent English Bibles have followed. The Hebrew range is more concrete than English "salvation": HALOT defines it as "help, salvation" — specifically divine help that rescues from a life-threatening situation. It is the word for being pulled from drowning, freed from siege, delivered from a hunting party. The later Christian theological term "salvation" is built on this base but loaded with doctrinal weight (eternal salvation, justification, sanctification) that the Hebrew text does not yet carry. Aperto renders *yəšûʿāh* as "rescue" throughout the Psalter to keep the Hebrew's concrete edge. The same word will appear in v.7 ("Rescue me!") and v.8 ("Rescue belongs to the LORD") — three appearances of the same word, marking the psalm's structural argument.\f* for him in God."

\b
\q1
\v 3 But you, LORD —\f + \fr 3:3 \fk TEXT \fq But you, LORD \ft The Hebrew is *we-ʾattāh YHWH* — a contra-accented emphatic pivot that NICOT identifies as the rhetorical hinge of the entire psalm. After the flood of "many, many, many" (vv.1–2), the singular "YOU" of God erupts. The ratio — all those enemies versus one God — is the psalm's theological claim made grammatically. NICOT: "The psalmist confesses her ongoing faith in the Lord (You), and, thus, balances the many who threaten her with the one you in whom she trusts." NICOT's choice of "she/her" for the psalmist throughout is not a marginal gesture — it is an exegetical claim that the speaking "I" of the psalm is available to anyone in crisis, regardless of gender, and refuses the centuries of default-male reading that have narrowed the psalm's pastoral reach.\f*
\q1 a shield around me,\f + \fr 3:3 \fk KULTUR \fq a shield around me \ft The Hebrew phrase is *māgēn baʿădî* — "shield all around me." The preposition *baʿad* indicates surrounding protection, not directional defense. BDB and HALOT confirm: this is not a hand-held shield held in front of the body but an encircling protective field. The image is precisely calibrated against the enemies of v.6 who are *sābîb* — "all around" the psalmist. Enemies surround on every side; God surrounds on every side. Older translations render the line "a shield for me" — losing the encirclement; most modern English translations similarly default to "my shield" without the "around." The geometry of the image is theologically decisive. — In Late Bronze Age Canaanite religion (documented by Mark Smith, *The Origins of Biblical Monotheism*, Oxford 2001), "shield" was a royal-divine title applied to El and other high gods. Psalm 3 takes the royal-warrior imagery and makes it intimate: not a shield over the king's army but a shield around one suffering individual.\f*
\q1 my honor,\f + \fr 3:3 \fk TEXT \fq my honor \ft The Hebrew word is *kəbôdî* — "my *kābôd*." For four hundred years, English Bibles have rendered this "my glory," following the KJV. That translation has a long shadow: "glory" in contemporary English is either churchy ("the glory of God"), military ("died in glory"), or ironic ("blaze of glory"). None of those carries what the Hebrew means. *Kābôd* — from a root meaning "weight" — is honor, social standing, the dignity a person carries in the eyes of their community. The enemies of vv.1–2 have publicly stripped the psalmist of standing, declaring him abandoned by God; the psalmist names YHWH as the source of the worth that has been taken away. Goldingay (2006): "Yhwh restores the honor as well as the person." Tim Keller (*The Songs of Jesus*, 2015) reframes this for contemporary readers: "David relocates his glory and hope to God." For a generation whose worth is constantly assessed by social-media verdict, professional performance, or popular approval, the line is direct: God, not their judgment, is the source of your standing.\f*
\q1 the one who lifts my head.\f + \fr 3:3 \fk KULTUR \fq the one who lifts my head \ft "Lifting the head" is an ancient Near Eastern reinstatement formula. In Akkadian royal documents, the king's head is "raised" after humiliation to mark restoration to honor. Hossfeld and Zenger: "The phrase 'lifting the head' is common across the ancient Near East. It cannot be restricted to legal contexts; it means encouragement, the laying aside of grief and fear." — The image is sharply specific to the Absalom story. Two chapters before the revolt, 2 Samuel 15:30 describes David's flight: "David went up the ascent of the Mount of Olives, weeping as he went, with his head covered, and walking barefoot." A covered head is a public sign of mourning, defeat, social erasure. The psalm's "the one who lifts my head" is the precise reversal: God uncovers what disgrace has covered, and the psalmist can look up again.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 4 I cry out to the LORD,
\q1 and he answers me from his holy mountain.\f + \fr 3:4 \fk KONTEXT \fq from his holy mountain \ft The "holy mountain" is Mount Zion — the temple mount in Jerusalem. Inside the Absalom-flight superscription, the psalmist is praying *away* from the temple, in flight, with the city behind him. 2 Samuel 15 places David crossing the Kidron Valley and ascending the Mount of Olives; the holy mountain he names in this verse is the one he can no longer reach. The answer comes anyway. The psalm's quiet claim is that the temple is not a transmission tower — God is not located there in a way that bars access from elsewhere. The mountain is named because that is where the cult places God, but the answer arrives on the road, in exile, away from any sacred building.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 5 I lay down. I slept.\f + \fr 3:5 \fk KULTUR \fq I lay down. I slept. \ft This is the most distinctive theological moment in Psalm 3. The Hebrew sequence *šākabtî wā-ʾîšānāh hĕqîṣôtî* — "I lay down, and I slept, I woke up" — has no parallel in ancient Near Eastern prayer literature. Mesopotamian *šu-ila* (hand-lifting) prayers maintain an urgent, awake, watchful posture before threatening deities; sleeping during danger would be spiritual negligence. Robert Alter (*The Book of Psalms*, Norton 2007): "the psalm's entire rhetorical force depends on the contrast between the 'many' enemies of vv.1–2 and the single trusting 'I' who sleeps while surrounded." — Benedict of Nursia (around 530 CE) chose Psalm 3 as the very first psalm of Matins — the opening prayer of every monastic day, the first thing prayed each week — precisely because a psalm that moves from "I am surrounded" to "I lay down and slept" is the right framework for prayer that begins honestly with real threats and arrives at trust, not denial. The Eastern Orthodox Church places it among the Six Psalms (*Hexapsalmoi*) read at every Matins service in darkness before the lighting of the lamps. The choice is ancient: begin the day with the prayer that does not pretend the threats away.\f*
\q1 I woke up — the LORD holds me up.\f + \fr 3:5 \fk LEBEN \fq I woke up — the LORD holds me up \ft These verbs are in the past tense in Hebrew. This matters pastorally. The line is not a promise about what God will do — it is testimony about what God did. Walter Brueggemann (*Praying the Psalms*, Cascade 2007) reads this through the lens of trauma-informed spirituality: the psalmist is not making a prospective claim ("I am sure God will keep me"); they are reporting a retrospective survival ("I slept and I woke up"). For someone who cannot believe in promises but can acknowledge what has already happened, this distinction is the difference between cheap reassurance and real testimony. — Brueggemann's larger framework distinguishes three modes of psalm-prayer: orientation (everything is well), disorientation (everything is not well), and reorientation (something has shifted, though circumstances remain). Psalm 3 sits in reorientation. The enemies are still there at v.6. The morning has come anyway. The psalmist has slept and woken, and that small fact is enough to anchor the rest of the day. — The Hebrew verb in the second half — *yismekēnî*, "holds me up" — is physical: it is the verb you use for a hand placed under a falling body. The line does not say "the LORD watches over me" or "the LORD comforts me." It says the LORD held me up, the way you would catch someone before they hit the ground.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 6 Ten thousand of them,\f + \fr 3:6 \fk TEXT \fq ten thousand of them \ft The Hebrew is *ribbəbôt ʿām* — "myriads of people." NICOT: the word *ribbəbôt* is built on the same Hebrew root as the *rabbîm* ("many") of vv.1–2. The threat has escalated — from "many" at the opening to "ten thousand" here — and yet the psalmist's fear has gone in the opposite direction. The same Hebrew root, opposite emotional effect: the wider the encirclement, the steadier the speaker. This is the psalm's most powerful rhetorical reversal, and it depends on the Hebrew root being audible across the verses. — In the same line, *sābîb* ("on every side") is the exact mirror of *baʿădî* ("around me") from v.3. Enemies all around; God all around. The geometry was already set up three verses ago.\f*
\q1 drawn up against me on every side.
\q1 I'm not afraid.

\b
\q1
\v 7 Arise, LORD!\f + \fr 3:7 \fk TEXT \fq Arise \ft The Hebrew imperative is *qûmāh* — "Arise!" — and it answers the enemies' *qāmîm* ("rising against me") of v.1. Same Hebrew root, reversed direction: you rose against me; now God rise against you. NICOT and Hossfeld–Zenger both name this lexical chiasm as the structural spine of the psalm's argument. — The verb has an even older history. Numbers 10:35 records the war-cry sung when Israel set out into the wilderness: "Arise, LORD! Let your enemies be scattered!" — the formula spoken as the ark moved out. Psalm 3 takes that ancient war-cry and democratizes it: any individual surrounded by enemies can pray it. The Tokunboh Adeyemo *Africa Bible Commentary* (Zondervan, 2006) makes this explicit: the verse is "the people's prayer that has become one person's prayer."\f*
\q1 Rescue me, my God!
\q1 You struck them in the jaw —
\q2 all my enemies.
\q1 You broke the teeth of the wicked.\f + \fr 3:7 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq you broke the teeth of the wicked \ft This verse — and verses like it across the Psalter — has been the focus of one of the most consequential debates in modern liturgy. Many liberal Protestant lectionaries omit verses like this; the Eastern Orthodox and Roman Catholic traditions retain them. Erich Zenger's *A God of Vengeance? Understanding the Psalms of Divine Wrath* (Westminster John Knox, 1996) is the definitive defense for keeping them. Zenger argues that removing such verses from communal prayer is a *refusal of hospitality to the suffering*. For someone genuinely surrounded — by violence, by systemic injustice, by abusive power — the imprecatory language is not primitive bloodlust but the articulation of the hunger for justice when earthly systems have failed. These psalms name what no polite prayer is allowed to name. — Zenger also notes the structural logic: the enemies *spoke* the verdict in v.2 ("there is no rescue for him in God"). The psalmist asks God to break their *teeth* — the instruments of their speech. The image is targeted, not random: it is the silencing of false speech, not gratuitous violence. Hossfeld–Zenger (Hermeneia, 2005): "the instruments of their speaking were destroyed." — A harm-history note: the early Christian commentator Ammonius of Alexandria (preserved in the *Ancient Christian Commentary on Scripture*) read "the wicked" whose teeth God breaks as "especially the Jews on account of unfaithfulness." That antisemitic reading inscribed an entire community into the role of "the wicked" and has caused real damage across the centuries. Modern commentary (NICOT, Goldingay, Hossfeld–Zenger, Theodoret of Cyr in his late-antique reading) is unanimous: "the wicked" are the specific opponents of the specific psalmist — not an ethnic, religious, or national category. The line cannot be read as collective verdict against any people group, and the historical use of it that way is one of the documented harms the psalm has carried.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 8 Rescue belongs to the LORD.\f + \fr 3:8 \fk KONTEXT \fq Rescue belongs to the LORD \ft The Hebrew is *layhwh hayšûʿāh* — literally, "to YHWH the rescue." The same Hebrew word, *yəšûʿāh*, that the enemies denied for the psalmist in v.2 ("there is no *yəšûʿāh* for him in God") is here declared, in the psalm's last syllables, to belong to YHWH alone. Same word; reversed ownership. This is the psalm's structural argument compressed into a single phrase. Mays: the verdict "there is no rescue for him in God" is not just an attack on the psalmist; it is an attempt to limit who counts as a recipient of God's care. The closing line is the rebuttal: rescue belongs to YHWH, which means YHWH's rescue is not foreclosed for anyone the powerful have written off. — Psalm 3 and Psalm 4 form a morning/evening diptych in the canonical order: this psalm contains the line "I lay down and slept; I woke up" (v.5); Psalm 4 closes with "I will lie down and sleep in peace" (4:8). J. Clinton McCann (in *The Shape and Shaping of the Book of Psalms*, SBL Press 2014) reads the pairing as deliberate — together they constitute the oldest recoverable daily prayer rhythm in the Psalter tradition: name real enemies in the morning, rest in peace at night.\f*
\q1 Your blessing — on your people.
